// tests/kernel/drivers/cam/hw_smoke_ipu6_placeholder.pdx — R40.M1-004 (#1350)
//
// DORMANT hardware-gated placeholder for the R40.M1 IPU6 firmware
// bring-up smoke on a real ThinkPad T14 G4.
//
// #1350 delivers `tools/hw-smoke-r40-ipu6.md`: an operator recipe
// for asserting that the R40.M1 wire (IPU6 PCI probe + firmware
// dual-sig verify + firmware upload + BOOT_READY handshake) works
// against a real IPU6 image processor and its shipping firmware
// bundle.  Per design/roadmap/next-wave-synthesis.md §10 D7
// (gated:hardware discipline), an issue whose acceptance requires
// PHYSICAL hardware in the loop CANNOT emit a golden fingerprint
// from a QEMU-OVMF boot -- QEMU has no IPU6 transport, no on-chip
// SRAM to shovel firmware into, no BOOT_READY interrupt to wait
// for, and the fingerprint would lie.
//
// The RIGHT SHAPE for a dormant issue is a placeholder module that
// COMPILES but emits NOTHING.  This module is that placeholder.  It
// carries no witness function, no fingerprint, and no boot-witness
// registration.  The build-time coverage gate is satisfied because
// there is nothing to cover.
//
// When R40.M2+ or a later milestone lands the IPU6 feature smoke on
// real hardware, this file is replaced by a real synth that emits
// "IPU6 HW-SMOKE OK" and takes ITS marker into the golden.
//
// The empty module is deliberately unreferenced from
// r30_platform.pdx.  That is the whole point of the dormant shape:
// nothing at boot calls into it, so nothing at boot lies about it.

module HwSmokeIpu6Placeholder = structure {
  // Compile-only marker so paideia-as accepts an "empty" module.
  pub let _r40_m1_004_hw_smoke_ipu6_placeholder : u64 = 0
}
